# meeting-copilot settings. Updating meeting-copilot never touches this file.
#
# One KEY=VALUE per line; # starts a comment; ~ and $VARS expand in paths.
# Every line below is commented out and shows the default, so this file changes
# nothing until you remove the # in front of a line and edit its value.
# A key with nothing after = means its default.
# A MEETING_COPILOT_* environment variable wins over the same key here.
# Environment only (not read from this file): MEETING_COPILOT_CONFIG (another
# file instead of this one), MEETING_COPILOT_LOG, MEETING_COPILOT_PYTHON.

# Muesli database, opened read-only.
#MEETING_COPILOT_DB=~/Library/Application Support/Muesli/muesli.db

# Local port. The server listens on 127.0.0.1 (see MEETING_COPILOT_LAN for the home network).
#MEETING_COPILOT_PORT=4390

# Path to the Claude Code CLI. Default: `claude` on your PATH.
#MEETING_COPILOT_CLAUDE=claude

# Optional wrapper for the AI calls (monthly API credit first, subscription when the API refuses).
# It comes from https://github.com/lucas-saldanha-werneck/api-credit
# Used only when this command is installed (on PATH or in ~/.local/bin). `off` never uses it.
#MEETING_COPILOT_API_CREDIT=claude-credito

# Model for suggestions, and for the fact search. DATA_MODEL left commented out
# follows MODEL; do not set it to an empty value.
#MEETING_COPILOT_MODEL=sonnet
#MEETING_COPILOT_DATA_MODEL=sonnet

# Folders the AI may search for facts, separated by : (; on Windows). Empty: none.
#MEETING_COPILOT_SEARCH_DIRS=

# An MCP config file ({"mcpServers": {...}}) for the fact search, and the
# comma-separated allowlist of its tools, e.g. mcp__notes__search. Without the
# allowlist MCP stays off. Only allow read-only tools.
#MEETING_COPILOT_MCP_CONFIG=
#MEETING_COPILOT_MCP_TOOLS=

# Fast fact search (needs the TypeSafe key, see TYPESAFE_KEY_FILE below, and SEARCH_DIRS):
# Jev (TypeSafe) says whether the new part of the conversation asks for a fact and which
# words name it, a text search (ripgrep when installed) finds the best lines in the search
# folders, Jev picks one, and only when Jev is unsure one call to a small model (no tools)
# reads those lines. The AI with tools (DATA_MODEL) runs only when this cannot answer.
# The new lines of the conversation and the lines found go to TypeSafe.
# fast or tools (only the AI with tools). Default: fast when the key and folders are set.
#MEETING_COPILOT_FACT_MODE=
# Jev must be at least this sure (0 to 1) that a fact is asked, else nothing is searched.
#MEETING_COPILOT_FACT_GATE_MIN=0.5
# Jev's pick is shown as is (the line and its file) when Jev is at least this sure.
#MEETING_COPILOT_FACT_PICK_MIN=0.8
# The small model that reads the lines when Jev is less sure. off: the AI with tools then.
#MEETING_COPILOT_FACT_CHECK_MODEL=haiku
# Its effort (low, medium, high, xhigh, max; empty: claude's default).
#MEETING_COPILOT_FACT_CHECK_EFFORT=low
# 1: the next small-model call is started ahead, so it does not wait for claude to start.
#MEETING_COPILOT_FACT_WARM=1
# How many lines Jev and the small model see.
#MEETING_COPILOT_FACT_SNIPPETS=10
# File name patterns the text search skips, separated by commas.
#MEETING_COPILOT_FACT_SKIP=*.mbox,*.gz

# A text file about you that both AI calls read (name, role, projects, where your
# notes are, words the transcript gets wrong). The first 8000 characters are used.
#MEETING_COPILOT_CONTEXT_FILE=

# Language for every suggestion, fact and Look answer, e.g. Brazilian Portuguese.
# Empty: the meeting's language. The language menu on the page changes it while
# the copilot runs (Auto, English, Portuguese, Spanish, French, German, Italian,
# or this value); that choice is kept in language.json in the data folder and
# wins over this line until you pick again or change this line.
#MEETING_COPILOT_LANGUAGE=

# A text file that replaces the built-in suggestions prompt (the "Say this" /
# "Ask this" call). {language} becomes your LANGUAGE (e.g. "answer in
# {language}") and {context} your CONTEXT_FILE text; whatever the file does not
# place is added at the end.
# Keep asking for say and ask (see README, "Your own prompt"). Missing or
# unreadable: one note in the log, and the built-in prompt.
# Empty: the built-in prompt.
#MEETING_COPILOT_PROMPT_FILE=

# The same for the fact-search prompt (the "Relevant facts" call). Also takes
# {sources}: the folders and tools it may search (added at the end if the file
# does not place it). Keep the word EMPTY: a reply that starts with it means
# "nothing found". Empty: the built-in prompt.
#MEETING_COPILOT_DATA_PROMPT_FILE=

# Command that opens the page. The URL goes in as one argument where {url} is,
# or at the end, e.g. open -g -a "Firefox"; leave {url} without quotes around it.
# Empty: open (macOS) or xdg-open.
#MEETING_COPILOT_OPEN_CMD=

# Extra host names allowed to load the page (behind your own reverse proxy or
# tunnel), comma-separated. Empty: none.
#MEETING_COPILOT_EXTRA_HOSTS=
# The header your tunnel fills with each visitor's real address, e.g. Cf-Connecting-IP
# for Cloudflare Tunnel (Cloudflare overwrites it, so a visitor cannot fake it). Used
# only for the pairing code's 5 tries per device, and only on requests to EXTRA_HOSTS.
# Name a header only if your tunnel always overwrites it. Empty: every visitor through
# the tunnel counts as one device.
#MEETING_COPILOT_CLIENT_IP_HEADER=

# 1: also answer your other devices on the home network: a second listener on
# 0.0.0.0:LAN_PORT that refuses every address outside the private ranges, with the
# same token. It answers other devices only on the networks in LAN_TRUSTED. Default: 0.
#MEETING_COPILOT_LAN=0
# The networks where the LAN page answers your other devices, as subnets separated by
# commas, e.g. 192.168.1.0/24 (`ipconfig getifaddr en0` on the Mac shows its address
# at home). Both this Mac and the device must be in one of them. On any other network
# (a hotel, an airport, a cafe) the LAN page refuses every device but this Mac, and the
# log says why. any: every private network (the rule before 0.17.0). Empty: no other
# device.
#MEETING_COPILOT_LAN_TRUSTED=
# Port of that listener (80 = no port in the URL; macOS lets your user use it).
#MEETING_COPILOT_LAN_PORT=80
# Name published as <name>.local with macOS dns-sd, e.g. http://copilot.local
# off: not published (use the LAN IP URL that `meeting-copilot status` prints).
#MEETING_COPILOT_LAN_NAME=copilot
# A fixed LAN address to publish. Empty: found by itself and followed when it changes.
#MEETING_COPILOT_LAN_IP=
# The dns-sd command that publishes the name. Default: dns-sd (macOS only; elsewhere
# no name is published).
#MEETING_COPILOT_DNS_SD=dns-sd
# 1: also https on the home network (needed for the phone camera: a phone's browser
# only opens the camera on an https page). The copilot makes a small certificate
# authority of its own that can vouch only for <LAN_NAME>.local and home network
# addresses, signs one certificate with it and deletes the authority's key at once.
# Each phone installs it once from http://<LAN_NAME>.local/setup (see README,
# "Phone camera"). While it runs, the plain http page only does that set-up and sends
# a set-up phone on to https: no login and no cookie over http.
# Default: 1 when LAN is 1 and CAMERA or LOOK is 1, else 0.
#MEETING_COPILOT_LAN_HTTPS=
# Port of the https listener (443 = no port in the URL; macOS lets your user use it).
#MEETING_COPILOT_LAN_HTTPS_PORT=443
# The openssl command that makes the certificate (empty: openssl on PATH; macOS has one).
#MEETING_COPILOT_OPENSSL=
# The command that keeps the certificate folder out of Time Machine backups
# (empty: tmutil on PATH, macOS only; off: not used).
#MEETING_COPILOT_TMUTIL=

# 1: no AI call in a meeting until you click "Turn on AI" on the page. Each
# meeting asks again. Useful when the copilot starts at login. Default: 0.
#MEETING_COPILOT_CONFIRM_AI=0
# minutes a "next meeting" click waits for a meeting (0 = no limit)
#MEETING_COPILOT_NEXT_MEETING_ARM_MIN=0

# 1: one `claude` process stays open per meeting for the suggestions, so a round
# has no start-up cost; it starts when the AI is allowed (the "Turn on AI" click,
# also for the next meeting, or the meeting start) and closes when it is not.
# 0: a new `claude -p` per round. With the API_CREDIT wrapper installed the
# kept-open process follows its policy (API credit first, subscription when the
# API refuses). Default: 1.
#MEETING_COPILOT_PERSISTENT_AI=1
# Watchdog of a suggestions round on that process, in seconds: no first byte from
# the API after AI_FIRST_BYTE_S, or no answer after AI_ROUND_S, and the process is
# replaced by a fresh one and the round tried once more.
#MEETING_COPILOT_AI_FIRST_BYTE_S=6
#MEETING_COPILOT_AI_ROUND_S=10

# Run the AI calls (suggestions, fact search, Look) on another machine, with this
# machine's claude as the fallback while that one does not answer (see README,
# "Run the AI on a server"). The command that starts claude there; the claude
# arguments go after it, quoted for the remote shell when it starts with ssh.
# Empty: everything runs here.
#MEETING_COPILOT_REMOTE=ssh myserver docker exec -i copilot-ai claude
# local=remote folder pairs, separated like SEARCH_DIRS: a search folder under a
# local folder is searched under the remote one; a search folder under none is
# searched only when the AI runs here.
#MEETING_COPILOT_REMOTE_PATHS=~/Dropbox=/notes
# The MCP config file on the remote machine (MCP_TOOLS applies to it). Empty: no
# MCP tools while the AI runs there.
#MEETING_COPILOT_REMOTE_MCP_CONFIG=
# A one-shot call there that prints nothing for this many seconds goes here, and
# how often (seconds, doubling after each failed check) the remote machine is
# checked again while it is out.
#MEETING_COPILOT_REMOTE_FIRST_BYTE_S=6
#MEETING_COPILOT_REMOTE_RETRY_S=30
# Where the fast fact search's text search (ripgrep) runs. remote: on that machine,
# one call per round over its copy of the search folders (REMOTE_PATHS), so a busy
# Mac does not slow it down; needs ripgrep there (tools/remote/Dockerfile has it).
# That copy is only as new as its last sync (sync-notes.sh: every 10 minutes), so
# the files changed here in the last FACT_RECENT_MIN minutes are searched here at the
# same time (this machine's lines win for them). local: everything here.
# Default: remote when REMOTE and REMOTE_PATHS are set.
#MEETING_COPILOT_FACT_SEARCH=
# Minutes: with the remote search, files changed here this recently are also searched
# here. 0: off (only the server copy).
#MEETING_COPILOT_FACT_RECENT_MIN=15
# Seconds the remote search may take; slower, failed (or ripgrep missing there) and
# this round and the next ones for REMOTE_RETRY_S search here instead.
#MEETING_COPILOT_FACT_SEARCH_REMOTE_S=3
# The command that starts ripgrep there. Empty: REMOTE with its last word claude
# made rg (ssh myserver docker exec -i copilot-ai rg).
#MEETING_COPILOT_REMOTE_RG=

# Intervals in seconds: suggestions, fact search, reading the transcript.
#MEETING_COPILOT_ASSIST_S=25
#MEETING_COPILOT_DATA_S=45
#MEETING_COPILOT_POLL_S=5

# Data folder (log, pid file). Default: $XDG_DATA_HOME/meeting-copilot,
# or ~/.local/share/meeting-copilot.
#MEETING_COPILOT_DATA_DIR=

# Where each meeting's transcript and suggestions are saved when it ends
# (default: the sessions folder inside the data folder). 0 turns saving off.
#MEETING_COPILOT_SESSIONS_DIR=
#MEETING_COPILOT_SAVE_SESSIONS=1

# json (default): everything, with the transcript. markdown: a readable
# "<Muesli export name>-copilot.md" with the suggestions only, named after
# Muesli's own auto-export (waits up to MUESLI_WAIT_S seconds for it).
#MEETING_COPILOT_SAVE_FORMAT=json
#MEETING_COPILOT_MUESLI_WAIT_S=600

# 1: the AI may also look at the meeting screen. Nothing is captured until you click
# "Share screen with AI" on the page, and each meeting asks again. Default: 0.
# macOS asks once for the Screen Recording permission (see README, "Screen and camera").
#MEETING_COPILOT_SCREEN=0
# window: only the meeting app's window (Zoom, Teams, FaceTime, Webex, a Slack huddle, or a
# browser window whose active tab is Google Meet/Teams). full: the whole main display.
#MEETING_COPILOT_SCREEN_MODE=window
# Seconds between two screen captures (a frame that did not change is not kept).
#MEETING_COPILOT_SCREEN_S=30
# More apps whose windows count as the meeting, comma-separated: an app name as macOS
# shows it, or a word in the window title. Empty: only the built-in list.
#MEETING_COPILOT_SCREEN_APPS=
# 1: keep the frames on disk after the meeting ends (in <data folder>/frames). Default: 0
# (deleted when the meeting ends; never written into the saved session).
#MEETING_COPILOT_KEEP_FRAMES=0
# The macOS commands used for the screen (empty: found on PATH; off: not used).
#MEETING_COPILOT_SCREENCAPTURE=
#MEETING_COPILOT_SIPS=
# A command that prints the windows on screen as JSON (id, owner, name, layer, w, h).
# Empty: a few lines of JXA run by osascript.
#MEETING_COPILOT_WINDOW_LIST=

# 1: the AI may also look through your phone's camera (a whiteboard, a paper, the room).
# A live meeting gets a "Camera" button on the page; nothing is sent until you tap it on
# the phone, and each meeting asks again. Live video needs https (LAN_HTTPS, or a tunnel
# with https); on a plain http page the button takes one photo per tap. Default: 0.
#MEETING_COPILOT_CAMERA=0
# Seconds between two looks at the camera picture; a frame goes to the Mac only when the
# picture changed (at least 2).
#MEETING_COPILOT_CAMERA_S=5
# 1: Look mode: a "Look" button on the page, no meeting needed. Point the phone camera at
# something (often the Mac screen) and get insights, answers and next steps; type a
# question for an answer at once. Nothing is sent before you tap Look; frames are deleted
# when it stops (see README, "Look mode"). Default: the same as CAMERA.
#MEETING_COPILOT_LOOK=
# Most often a changed picture is looked at by the AI in Look mode, seconds (at least 3).
#MEETING_COPILOT_LOOK_S=3
# Seconds between two looks at the camera picture in Look mode (at least 2); as for
# CAMERA_S, a frame goes to the Mac only when the picture changed, so a still or noisy
# scene makes no AI call.
#MEETING_COPILOT_LOOK_CAMERA_S=2
# With no change seen for this many seconds, the next frame (the page re-sends an
# unchanged picture once a minute) goes to the AI anyway, so a change too small to be
# seen is not missed. 0: never.
#MEETING_COPILOT_LOOK_REFRESH_S=60
# 1: one `claude` is started ahead and waits for the next Look round, so a round does
# not wait for its start (5-8 s on a busy Mac). Each round is still a new conversation.
#MEETING_COPILOT_LOOK_WARM=1
# Longest one Look call may take, seconds. A call takes 6-9 s on a quiet Mac; each one
# starts a new `claude`, and on a Mac short of memory that start alone can pass 30 s.
# When the time runs out, the page says so with the Mac's load.
#MEETING_COPILOT_LOOK_TIMEOUT_S=90
# Look in two passes. A fast model answers every round (empty: MODEL).
#MEETING_COPILOT_LOOK_FAST_MODEL=haiku
# A deep model re-checks in the background and adds items only to correct or complete the
# fast answer (shown with a "deep check" label; a corrected item is struck through). It runs
# when the gate says a question is on the screen, or when you type a question. Empty or
# off: no deep pass (Look is then the fast model alone). Default: opus.
#MEETING_COPILOT_LOOK_DEEP_MODEL=opus
# jev: ask TypeSafe's Jev (a typed yes/no model, about US$0.0001 per call, 1-3 s, in
# parallel with the fast model) whether the newest picture shows a question or task waiting
# for an answer. off: no gate; the deep pass then runs only for a typed question.
# Default: jev when a TypeSafe key is set (below), else off. Needs a TypeSafe API key.
#MEETING_COPILOT_LOOK_GATE=
# The deep pass runs when Jev's probability is at least this (0 to 1).
#MEETING_COPILOT_LOOK_GATE_MIN=0.8
# The TypeSafe key for the gate and the fast fact search: put it in the environment of the copilot as
# MEETING_COPILOT_TYPESAFE_KEY, or in a file (first line, chmod 600; a file that others can
# read is not used) and name the file here. The key is never written in this file's values,
# the log, the page or the AI's command line or environment.
#MEETING_COPILOT_TYPESAFE_KEY_FILE=
